  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1229 PM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nUnfortunately, the heat will remain the theme through the extended \nforecast as the upper level ridge is in no hurry to exit the region. \nInitially somewhat elongated from west-to-east late week, the ridge \nis forecast to rebuild more squarely over the central and southern \nHigh Plains late this weekend where it will likely linger through \nmuch or all of next week. This will ensure the scorching \ntemperatures remain entrenched over West Texas, and additional Heat \nAdvisories are a good likelihood for at least parts of the area \nthrough the extended. \n\nThe only hope for any meaningful precipitation and perhaps minor \nheat relief will be late Friday into Saturday. This will occur as \nthe shortwave discussed in the Short Term, exiting via the central \nMississippi River Valley, bends a thinning plume of monsoonal \nmoisture across the Texas Panhandle and northern South Plains. The \nincreased moisture aloft, in combination with convergence along a \nsurface trough and intense heating, will likely trigger scattered \nhigh-based storms across our northwestern/northern zones Friday \nevening. The hot and deeply mixed sub-cloud layer will support \nsevere-caliber winds, while the improved moisture aloft could also \nbring a few pockets of heavy rainfall. The increased cloud cover and \ndeep-layer moisture may knock down temperatures a few degrees on \nSaturday. Overall moisture aloft will gradually thin on Saturday,\nbut could still be sufficient to support a low thunder risk again\nSaturday afternoon/evening. Thereafter, with the mid-level \nmoisture depleted, dry conditions will likely be the rule through \nmuch or all of next week. In addition, temperatures will rebound \nfrom the subtle cooling, with widespread highs in the triple \ndigits next week.",
